# it writes t//c for the operand t/ (a second slash); here a name is joined to a directory that ends in a slash with no second one
-a t/
t/
t/a/
# with -L it counts a directory again each time a link leads to it, and a link that leads nowhere as the link, without an error; the standard counts a file with more than one link (a directory has at least two) once, and says that du reports an error when it cannot stat a file
-aHL t
-aL t
-H -L t
-HkL
-kL
-L t
-sL t
-H t/dang
-L t/dang
